---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/drip-alternative';
const title = "Drip alternatives for Shopify: repair or rebuild?";
const desc = "Decide whether to simplify Drip workflows, retain email with a separate SMS service, or rebuild with a documented branch and queue handover.";
const faqs = [{"question": "When is staying on Drip sensible?", "answer": "When its email workflows meet the brief and an owner can maintain the event and tag definitions. Repair one problematic journey before budgeting a replacement of the entire library."}, {"question": "Does Drip support SMS now?", "answer": "Drip advertises a phased US rollout for existing paid customers with Support All People enabled. Confirm access for your account. Its launch offer requires an email for each phone record and uses one-way toll-free sending."}, {"question": "Can tags substitute for permission?", "answer": "No. Preserve what was agreed to, for which sender, purpose and channel, with source and timestamp, plus current withdrawals. A tag describes membership; it does not establish permission to text."}, {"question": "Can a workflow screenshot serve as the migration plan?", "answer": "It is a useful reference, but also record event payloads, branch precedence, tag writers, template fields, waiting people and queued sends. The incoming platform needs an explicit decision for each of these."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container max-w-4xl">
    <nav class="mb-8"><a class="link" href="/alternatives">Alternatives</a></nav>
    <h1 class="hero-title">{title}</h1>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-12">
      <p>Drip offers visual email workflows, reusable templates and dynamic audiences based on behavior and purchases. Those are useful reasons to stay when the store needs different education and follow-up paths. A confusing workflow library is a reason to inspect ownership and logic before buying another builder. <a class="link" href="https://www.drip.com/ai-info">Drip's current product scope</a> includes welcome, cart, post-purchase and win-back templates; these jobs do not inherently require a replacement.</p>
      <h2>Separate the email decision from the SMS decision</h2>
      <p>According to <a class="link" href="https://www.drip.com/sms">Drip's SMS launch page</a>, access is rolling out in phases to existing US-based paid customers with Support All People enabled. Phone records need an email; SMS-only subscribers are unsupported. Launch sending is toll-free and one-way, without keyword enrollment, short codes or conversational replies. Confirm eligibility and activation before designing a live journey. A draft builder is not evidence that an account can send.</p>
      <p>Keep Drip email if it serves the program, even when a separate texting provider is needed for a documented requirement such as reply handling or phone-only acquisition. That split needs an integration owner: which system receives opt-outs, where replies become support tickets, and who prevents a recovery text after a purchase? Replacing email solely to obtain a different SMS capability adds a second migration problem.</p>
      <h2>Repair a branch before replacing the library</h2>
      <p>Choose one real journey, such as first-order education followed by replenishment. Record the entry event and example payload, customer identity, every delay, yes/no decision, exit condition and template. For every tag, list its meaning, all systems that write it and whether it expires. A campaign label from a past sale should not silently behave as a permanent VIP qualification.</p>
      <p>Draw the intended path for a new buyer, returning buyer, gift purchaser and subscriber to a product plan. Compare that map with the active workflow. Remove redundant branches only after checking downstream consumers of the tags. Keep separate timing for gifts and consumable replenishment when the store has evidence for those differences. Assign a maintainer and record why each remaining branch exists.</p>
      <p>As a proposed exercise, introduce a purchase during a delay, an event replay and an inventory change before a cart reminder. Define the expected exit or revised message before running it. Include a buyer with an open service ticket and a recent withdrawal. Inspect both eligibility and actual send logs; a visually correct branch does not prove that late data reaches it. These are acceptance exercises, not results observed on a merchant account.</p>
      <h2>Give replacement candidates the same rebuild brief</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="/alternatives/omnisend-alternative">Omnisend</a> is a candidate when the requirement is a connected Shopify capture and recovery operation. Its evaluation should include the form-to-order path, not just the email editor. For another visual email workflow option, compare <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-drip">Klaviyo and Drip</a> using the exact event payload and branch map. Do not choose by an assumed order-volume threshold.</p>
      <p>Ask each candidate to demonstrate the needed trigger, purchase checks, template fields, tag-to-segment mapping and handling of waiting people. Price the supported implementation and the labor to maintain it. A simpler brief may be satisfied by repairing Drip; a provider that cannot represent a required branch is a poor replacement regardless of its template count.</p>
      <h2>Transfer waiting people, not just workflow pictures</h2>
      <p>Before switching, export authorized contact data with channel-specific permission evidence and current suppressions. Save approved creative, reusable blocks, product fallbacks and links under the store's retention policy. Record whether imported historical orders can trigger enrollment and how event IDs will prevent replay duplicates.</p>
      <p>Stop old enrollment for the transferred purpose. Reconcile people in delays and already queued messages: finish under the old owner, cancel and resume at a named step, or deliberately exclude them. Record the choice per cohort rather than restarting everyone at welcome. Assign one sender per purpose and channel. At send time, check current purchase, withdrawal and suppression state. Keep a rollback owner able to stop the new queue; restoring old enrollment must not replay messages already sent.</p>
      <h2>Compare maintenance and message economics</h2>
      <p>Use an ordinary month and a sale month with the same contact population, email volume, SMS subscribers, destinations and message segments. Obtain current email and SMS quotes, including overages, registration, carrier charges where applicable and staff time. Drip prices SMS separately from email; its published offer scales by SMS audience and segments. Avoid comparing a contact tier with a per-send estimate at different volumes.</p>
      <p>For an illustrative $100 basket discounted 25%, receipts are $75. With fixed goods cost of $40 and $15 in fulfillment, payment and messaging costs, contribution is $20. With goods cost of $65, contribution is −$5. The discount alone cannot establish profitability. Twenty genuinely incremental orders at $20 contribution less $300 additional monthly operating cost would leave $100. Use a suitable holdout or other credible causal measurement rather than treating all attributed orders as incremental.</p>
      <p>Before a VIP drop or Black Friday, review inventory freshness, suppression delivery, support ownership and segment-level opt-outs. Set contact cadence from the promised experience and observed response; an arbitrary one-day gap between email and SMS does not solve shared eligibility. Keep the repaired Drip program if it passes the brief at an acceptable operating cost.</p>
    </article>
  </div></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip alternatives FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
